{"text": "Department of Homeland Security\nFROM: Ellen Gallagher, Senior Policy Advisor,\nCivil Rights and Civil Lit\nDATE: July 23,2014\nSUBJECT: \u2018The Use of Segregation for Immigration Detainees\n\n\u2018Summary: The purpose of this memorandum isto elevate for review by the Department of Homeland\n\u2018Security (DHS) Deputy Secretary the use administrative and disciplinary segregation for detainees in\nimmigration custody. Particularly with regard to detainees with \u201cspecial vulnerabilities,\u201d the Performance-\nBased National Detention Standards (PBNDS) in conjunction with ICE Directive 11065.1, \u201cReview of the\nUse of Segregation for ICE Detainees,\u201d require that segregation be used \u201conly asa last resort and when no\n\u2018ther viable housing options exist.\"' Implementation of this requirement necessitates a range of resources\napplied in general and other modified custodial settings that ICE repors is lacking. This has resulted in\nsegregation being used across detention facilites asa first, not lat resort, with \u201csentences\u201d of up to 60\nconsecutive days, or more. Segregation, also termed \u201ctwenty-four hour lockdown,\u201d is routinely applied to\nimmigration detainees for: \u201cViolations of the Rules/Regulations; Inappropriate Behavior; Self-Threatening\nBehavior; Posing a Threat to the Security of the Facility; and To Avoid Conflict Between Yourself and\nOthers.\"\u201d Some groups, including detainees with serious mental iliness or a prior sex offense, are placed\nautomaticaly in long-term administrative segregation. Others move chronically back and fort from the\n\u2018general population to administrative or disciplinary segregation, with periodic, crsis-oriented admissions to\npsychiatric hospitals punctuating their return to the same disturbing cycle. I have attempted to document and\nclevate within and beyond CRCL my concer regarding the widespread and at times extreme use of solitary\nconfinement for detainees in immigration custody. elie call alegre agli alta\n\nto imposing administrative or disciplinary segregation. In a meeting with Officer Megan H. Mack and other\n\u2018CRCL staff on July 10, 2014, I recommended that the Officer brief Secretary Jeh Johnson on this topic, and\nalso share available information on segregation to inform the DHS Office of the Inspector General's open\nreport (O1G-11-62) on \u201cManagement of Mental Health Cases in Immigration Detention.\u201d I respect the\nOffficer\u2019s decision not to pursue either course of action at this time, however, after consultations with Ethics\nCounsel and notice through my established chain of command, I feel obligated to seek your review.\n\nNature and Scope of Segregation: In accordance with ICE Directive 1106S.1, administrative segregation is\n\u201ca non-punitive form of separation from the general population for administrative reasons.\u201d* It is authorized\n\u201conly as necessary to ensure the safety of the detainee, facility staff, and other detainees; the protection of\nproperty or the security or good order of the facility, and therefore should be forthe briefest term and under\nthe least restrictive conditions practicable, consistent with the rationale for placement.\u201d* Disciplinary\nsegregation is\u201ca punitive form of separation from the general population that is authorized only pursuant to\nthe order ofa facility disciplinary panel, following a hearing in which the detainee is determined to have\n\u2018committed serious misconduct in violation ofa facility rule, and only consistent with the Disciplinary\n\n* See \u201cUS Immigration and Customs Enforcement's New Directive on Segregation: Why We Need Further Protections,\u201d\nJohn Marshall Law School (February 2014) at pp. 11-14 indicating sanctions from least to most restrictive.\n\u00ae See Douglas County Department of Corrections Documents re: Jat Attachment 5.\nUS. Immigration and Enforcement Directive 11065.1, Review of the Use of Segregation for ICE (DHS 2013) \u00a7 3.1.\n\u201cM",
